				"paragraphStyle" : {
					"__type" : "textStyle"
				},
				"string" : "# TTˆObject — a namespacing convention for Smalltalk\n\n## The idea\n\nA class can **know** that its name contains a namespace prefix and a local\nname, and can choose to render either. The convention uses `ˆ` as a separator\nbaked into the class name itself.\n\n- `self class name` returns the **full** name: `'TTˆObject'`.\n- `self class displayName` returns the **local** name: `'Object'`.\n- `self class namespace` returns `'TT'`.\n\nThe class **knows** which part is namespace and which is local name, because\nthe convention is encoded in its behaviour, not in an external parser.\n\n## Why this is new\n\nNo existing Smalltalk convention lets a class advertise its own name with\noptional namespace rendering. Class names are flat Symbols; the namespace\ninformation (if any) is external, in a registry, in a package, in a prefix.\n\nWith this convention, the namespace is part of the class's behaviour. A\nclass that mixes in `TTNamespaced` **is** a namespaced class, automatically.\n\n## The model\n\n```smalltalk\nObject subclass: #TTˆObject\n    uses: TTNamespaced\n    ...\n```\n\nwhere `TTNamespaced` is a trait (or mixin) with three accessors:\n\n```smalltalk\nTTNamespaced >> namespace [\n    ^ 'TT'\n]\n\nTTNamespaced >> localName [\n    ^ self name withoutPrefix: self namespace, 'ˆ'\n]\n\nTTNamespaced >> displayName [\n    ^ self localName\n]\n\nTTNamespaced >> fullName [\n    ^ self name\n]\n```\n\n## Properties\n\n1. **Identity is honest.** The class *is* `TTˆObject`. No aliasing, no\n   canonicalisation, no two-layer indirection.\n2. **Rendering is configurable.** Debug print can show either the full or\n   the local name; the choice is policy, not hard-coded.\n3. **The convention is enforced by behaviour.** Any class with\n   `TTNamespaced` is automatically a namespaced class. No external registry\n   to keep in sync.\n4. **Reflection still works.** `self class name` returns the full name;\n   `self class displayName` returns the local name; both are methods, both\n   can be overridden.\n5. **Tools that don't know about `ˆ`** continue to show `TTˆObject`, which\n   is the right answer, because that is the actual name. No loss of\n   information.\n\n## What to add to make it complete\n\n### A protocol for parsing\n\nGiven `'TTˆObject'`, extract `(namespace: 'TT', local: 'Object')`. Trivial:\nsplit on the first `ˆ`.\n\n```smalltalk\nString >> splitNamespaceAndLocal [\n    | i |\n    i := self indexOf: $ˆ.\n    ^ i = 0\n        ifTrue:  [ Array with: nil with: self ]\n        ifFalse: [ Array with: (self copyFrom: 1 to: i - 1)\n                        with: (self copyFrom: i + 1 to: self size) ]\n]\n```\n\n### A registry or search path\n\n```smalltalk\nTheHub class >> ensureClass: aString ifAbsent: aBlock [\n    \"Resolves 'ˆObject' by walking the registered namespaces.\n     Resolves 'TTˆObject' directly. Returns aBlock value if not found.\"\n    aString size = 0 ifTrue: [ ^ aBlock value ].\n\n    aString first = $ˆ ifFalse: [\n        \"qualified name: namespaceˆlocal\"\n        ^ self resolveQualified: aString ifAbsent: aBlock ].\n\n    \"unqualified: scan the active namespace search path\"\n    ^ self resolveUnqualified: aString allButFirst ifAbsent: aBlock\n]\n```\n\nEach namespace is just a class-side dictionary on a marker class, or a\nsingle global registry keyed by namespace name.\n\n### A naming policy\n\nWhen you create a new class in a namespace, do you auto-prefix `ˆ`, or\ndo you write `TTˆObject` manually?\n\n- **Auto-prefixing** is harder to implement but cleaner to use. Requires\n  intercepting class creation in the namespace.\n- **Manual** is what your code already does, and matches the Smalltalk\n  tradition of explicit naming.\n\nEither is consistent with the design.\n\n### Tonel export\n\nVerify that `TTˆObject` survives a `commit → pull → filein` cycle. The\nclass name is a Symbol whose bytes include the `ˆ` character (U+02C6);\nTonel writes UTF-8, so it should round-trip safely. Verify once before\ncommitting to the convention.\n\n## Sharp edge: ambiguity\n\nIf two namespaces both define `ˆFoo`, and you look up `'ˆFoo'`, which wins?\nYou need a deterministic rule. The recommended policy:\n\n- **No implicit resolution.** `'ˆFoo'` only works if *exactly one*\n  namespace is in scope.\n- **Explicit qualification:** `'TTˆFoo'` always works.\n- **Ambiguity is an error**, not a silent pick.\n\nThat keeps the convention honest. Unqualified names are convenient when\nthere is no ambiguity; qualified names are always safe.\n\n## Verdict\n\nThis is a coherent, small, principled design. The `ˆ` baked into the class\nname, with `displayName` / `fullName` separation enforced by a trait, is\ngenuinely a new affordance in Smalltalk. The price is paid in:\n\n- **Tool support**: browsers, refactoring tools, and code search don't\n  know about `ˆ`; they show the full name with `ˆ` in it, which is fine\n  but less pretty.\n- **Onboarding**: a one-paragraph note explaining the convention is\n  required for any new contributor.\n- **Encoding**: the `ˆ` must be the precomposed form (U+02C6) and survive\n  NFC normalisation across operating systems.\n\nThe price is **not** paid in correctness: the class identity, the\nreflection model, and the tool round-trip all work.\n"
			}
		]
	},
